<!-- v3 (2026-11-17, help overhaul): John's structure (intro, What it does,
     How to use this tool, then more) and the shared blocks in ui/css/help.css. -->
<p>Now and then an MP3 arrives damaged, often from a download that didn't finish cleanly. Audio Repair fixes the files MetaForge couldn't fix on its own, without ever putting the original at risk.</p>

<h3>What it does:</h3>
<p><strong class="tool">Unpack &amp; Convert</strong> and the <strong class="tool">Intelli-Tagger</strong> check every MP3 and fix small faults themselves. A file they can't fix is added to the list here, with the reason. Unpack &amp; Convert also adds a lossless file (FLAC, WAV and so on) when converting it to MP3 failed.</p>
<p>Audio Repair works on a copy of each file, checks the copy, and only then replaces the original.</p>

<section class="steps">
    <h3>How to use this tool:</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Look over <strong>Files waiting for repair</strong>. Each entry shows the file, its album and why it was added. <span class="ui-button">Remove</span> takes a file off the list without touching it, for example if you've replaced it yourself.</li>
        <li>Choose <span class="ui-button">Repair the files</span>, then <span class="ui-button">Repair</span> to confirm.</li>
        <li>Each file is repaired in turn. <span class="ui-button">Stop</span> ends the run after the current file; everything repaired so far is kept, and the rest stays on the list.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">If you switch tools during a run, MetaForge asks first.</p>
</section>

<h3>How a file is repaired</h3>
<ol>
    <li>MetaForge works on a <strong>copy</strong>, never on the file itself.</li>
    <li>An MP3 first has its damaged frames removed <strong>without re-encoding</strong>, so the sound quality and bitrate stay the same. If that isn't enough, the copy is re-encoded at V0 and the original's tags are copied onto it.</li>
    <li>A lossless file is converted to MP3 (V0) the same way Unpack &amp; Convert does it: same file name pattern, same tags. Run the Intelli-Tagger on that album afterwards.</li>
    <li>The copy is <strong>checked</strong>: it must play cleanly from start to finish and be within one second of the original's length.</li>
    <li>Only a copy that passes replaces the file. A copy that fails is thrown away, and the file stays on the list exactly as it was.</li>
</ol>
<p>Re-encoding can't add back quality that was never there: a 128 kbps file stays a 128 kbps recording, even at V0.</p>

<h3>Your originals</h3>
<p>Before a file is replaced, the original is copied to <code>%APPDATA%\MetaForge\backups\repair</code>. Each run gets its own folder, named with the date and time, then the album. Nothing there is deleted automatically: remove the folder when you're happy with the repairs.</p>
<p>Each repair is also written to the album's <code>MetaForge.log</code>.</p>

<p>See also: <button type="button" class="see-also" data-mf-help="unpack_convert" data-mf-help-name="Unpack &amp; Convert">Unpack &amp; Convert</button>.</p>
<!-- --- END OF FILE help.mfi --- -->
